WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.761
DRAGONN works by pre-allocating a small memory
buffer sized to the compression ratio.

00:00:05.841 --> 00:00:09.885
For each gradient whose absolute value clears the
threshold,

00:00:09.965 --> 00:00:14.283
it hashes the gradient's index to a slot and writes
it directly,

00:00:14.363 --> 00:00:17.719
rather than scanning to compute nonzero positions.

00:00:17.799 --> 00:00:22.392
If two indices collide, the later one simply
overwrites the earlier,

00:00:22.472 --> 00:00:24.935
and any slot left empty maps to zero.

00:00:25.015 --> 00:00:28.646
Because writing memory is an atomic operation on GPUs,

00:00:28.726 --> 00:00:34.213
many threads can hash and write at the same time
with no dependency between them,

00:00:34.293 --> 00:00:39.230
so the whole compression needs only d comparisons
plus l hash operations,

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:41.978
essentially the theoretical lower bound.

00:00:42.058 --> 00:00:43.215
To deploy it well,

00:00:43.295 --> 00:00:49.332
DRAGONN adds two tricks: it only compresses a
tensor when the compression time is smaller

00:00:49.412 --> 00:00:51.875
than the communication it would save,

00:00:51.955 --> 00:00:58.266
and it batches all the compressed tensors received
from other workers into one sparse decode,

00:00:58.346 --> 00:01:03.420
so decoding cost stays nearly constant no matter
how many GPUs participate.
